The tool below is called with `{ "query": "mug", "limit": 999 }`. What does the client get?

```ts
server.registerTool('search', {
  description: 'Search the catalog',
  inputSchema: z.object({ query: z.string(), limit: z.number().int().max(50).optional() })
}, async ({ query, limit }) => ({ content: [{ type: 'text', text: `${query}:${limit}` }] }));
```

The weather API is down and this handler throws. What does `await client.callTool({ name: 'forecast', arguments: { city: 'Oslo' } })` produce?

```ts
async ({ city }) => {
  const res = await fetch(`${API}/forecast?city=${city}`);
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`Weather API returned ${res.status}`);
  return { content: [{ type: 'text', text: await res.text() }] };
}
```

What does the client see when it calls `whoami` with no arguments?

```ts
server.registerTool('whoami', { description: 'Debug helper' }, async (args) => ({
  content: [{ type: 'text', text: Object.keys(args).sort().join(',') }]
}));
```

This tool declares an `outputSchema`. An upstream change makes `total` arrive as the string `'ten'`. What does the client receive?

```ts
server.registerTool('order-total', {
  inputSchema: z.object({ id: z.string() }),
  outputSchema: z.object({ total: z.number() })
}, async ({ id }) => {
  const total = await fetchTotal(id); // now returns 'ten'
  return { content: [{ type: 'text', text: String(total) }], structuredContent: { total } };
});
```

This shopping server is served with `createMcpHandler`. A client calls `add-item` with `mug`, then with `cup`. The second call returns `Cart: cup`. Why?

```ts
const handler = createMcpHandler(() => {
  const server = new McpServer({ name: 'shop', version: '1.0.0' });
  const cart: string[] = [];
  server.registerTool('add-item', { inputSchema: z.object({ sku: z.string() }) }, async ({ sku }) => {
    cart.push(sku);
    return { content: [{ type: 'text', text: `Cart: ${cart.join(', ')}` }] };
  });
  return server;
});
```

